  "text": "Let's break down the problem step by step:\n\n1. **Understanding the problem:**\n   We have a regular 24-gon (a 24-sided polygon with equal sides and angles). We need to draw 12 segments of equal lengths such that each vertex of the 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one segment. This means we need to partition the vertices of the 24-gon into 12 pairs, where each pair consists of two adjacent vertices (since the polygon is regular, no vertex can be an endpoint of more than one segment).\n\n2. **Counting the number of ways:**\n   For each pair of adjacent vertices, we can either include it in the set of segments or not. Since there are 12 pairs (since we are drawing 12 segments), and each pair can either be included or not included, there are \\(2^{12}\\) possible ways to draw the segments.\n\n3. **Calculating the number of ways:**\n   \\[\n   2^{12} = 4096\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths so that each vertex in the set \\(S\\) is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments is \\(\\boxed{4096}\\).",
